An API key is a unique secret string that allows one application to authenticate and access another application's API.
Think of it as a password for applications.

Why do we use API keys?
Authentication – Identifies who is making the request.
Authorization – Controls what the application is allowed to do.
Usage tracking – Measures how many requests are made.
Rate limiting – Prevents abuse by limiting requests.
Billing – Associates API usage with an account.

How it works
You sign up for an API service (e.g., OpenAI, Google Maps).
The service generates an API key for your account.
Your application includes the API key in every request.
The server validates the key and returns a response.

Client Application
        |
        |  API Request + API Key
        |
        V
   API Server
        |
   Validate Key
        |
   Return Response

Example

Suppose you're calling a weather API.

HTTP Request

GET /weather?city=Islamabad HTTP/1.1
Host: api.weather.com
Authorization: Bearer YOUR_API_KEY

Or sometimes:

GET https://api.weather.com/weather?apikey=YOUR_API_KEY


Java Spring Boot Example

HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setBearerAuth("YOUR_API_KEY");

HttpEntity<String> entity = new HttpEntity<>(headers);

RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();

ResponseEntity<String> response = restTemplate.exchange(
    "https://api.example.com/data",
    HttpMethod.GET,
    entity,
    String.class
);

System.out.println(response.getBody());


Where are API keys stored?

Never hardcode them in your source code.

Instead, store them in:

Environment variables
application.properties
application.yml
Secret managers (AWS Secrets Manager, Azure Key Vault, etc.)

Example:

openai.api.key=sk-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

Read it in Spring Boot:

@Value("${openai.api.key}")
private String apiKey;
API Key vs Username/Password
| API Key                     | Username & Password        |
| --------------------------- | -------------------------- |
| Used by applications        | Used by people             |
| Usually long random string  | Human-readable credentials |
| Sent with every API request | Used mainly during login   |
| Can be regenerated easily   | Usually changed manually   |

API Key vs JWT Token
| API Key                          | JWT Token                                     |
| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| Identifies the application       | Identifies the user                           |
| Long-lived                       | Usually expires after minutes or hours        |
| Doesn't contain user information | Contains user claims (ID, roles, permissions) |
| Generated manually from provider | Generated after successful login              |

Real-world example

Imagine your Spring Boot application needs to send SMS using a third-party SMS provider.

The SMS provider gives you:

API Key = abc123xyz

Your application sends:
POST /sendSMS
Authorization: Bearer abc123xyz

The SMS provider checks:
Is the key valid? ✅
Is the account active? ✅
Has the rate limit been exceeded? ❌
If everything is valid, the SMS is sent.

Best practices
✅ Keep API keys secret.
✅ Store them in environment variables or a secrets manager.
✅ Rotate keys periodically.
✅ Use HTTPS so keys aren't exposed in transit.
✅ Never commit API keys to Git repositories.
✅ Restrict key permissions to only what your application needs.

